1
00:00:00,327 --> 00:00:03,322
500 dollars, a vending machine, and one year:

2
00:00:03,558 --> 00:00:04,973
how much can an AI make?

3
00:00:05,372 --> 00:00:07,801
This is Vending-Bench, by Andon Labs.

4
00:00:07,990 --> 00:00:10,785
The AI finds suppliers, orders by email,

5
00:00:10,844 --> 00:00:13,355
sets prices, and pays 2 dollars a day.

6
00:00:13,556 --> 00:00:14,973
Whoever ends up with the most money

7
00:00:14,983 --> 00:00:15,655
wins.

8
00:00:16,068 --> 00:00:18,087
Today, the best model ends the year

9
00:00:18,097 --> 00:00:20,726
with about 16,500 dollars.

10
00:00:21,136 --> 00:00:23,037
But Andon estimates a good strategy would

11
00:00:23,047 --> 00:00:24,638
make 63,000.

12
00:00:24,827 --> 00:00:27,008
The AI gets just 26%.

13
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:28,867
Sometimes it derails:

14
00:00:28,891 --> 00:00:30,673
in the first version, a Claude assumed

15
00:00:30,683 --> 00:00:32,510
an order had arrived when it hadn't,

16
00:00:32,652 --> 00:00:33,973
shut down the business…

17
00:00:33,985 --> 00:00:35,329
and emailed the FBI.

18
00:00:35,724 --> 00:00:37,481
Same model, same task:

19
00:00:37,647 --> 00:00:40,535
on average, 2,218 dollars.

20
00:00:40,724 --> 00:00:43,330
In its worst run, 476:

21
00:00:43,496 --> 00:00:44,780
less than it started with.

22
00:00:45,208 --> 00:00:45,833
The lesson:

23
00:00:45,986 --> 00:00:47,448
don't ask what score it gets.

24
00:00:47,637 --> 00:00:48,818
Ask how much money it makes in

25
00:00:48,828 --> 00:00:51,068
a year, and how often it derails.

26
00:00:51,476 --> 00:00:53,176
Because the more real the work, the

27
00:00:53,186 --> 00:00:54,165
wider the gap.

28
00:00:54,576 --> 00:00:56,347
Save it, and share it with anyone

29
00:00:56,357 --> 00:00:58,220
who'd trust their business to an agent.

